 This problem is a well-known application of the Schwarz lemma in complex analysis. Let us first set $\gamma = \frac{\beta - \alpha}{1 - \overline{\alpha} \beta}.$ We aim to show that $|\gamma| \leq 1$ and then determine when equality holds. Note that $|\beta| = 1$ implies $\beta\overline{\beta} = 1.$ Thus, $\beta = \frac{1}{\overline{\beta}}.$ Plugging this into the expression for $\gamma$, we obtain
\begin{align*} \gamma = \frac{\beta - \alpha}{1 - \overline{\alpha} \beta} &= \frac{\frac{1}{\overline{\beta}} - \alpha}{1 - \overline{\alpha} \cdot \frac{1}{\overline{\beta}}} \\ &= \frac{1 - \overline{\beta} \alpha}{\overline{\beta} - \overline{\alpha}}. \end{align*}
Taking the absolute value of both numerator and denominator, we get
\begin{align*} |\gamma| &= \left| \frac{1 - \overline{\beta} \alpha}{\overline{\beta} - \overline{\alpha}} \right| \\ &= \frac{|1 - \overline{\beta} \alpha|}{|\overline{\beta} - \overline{\alpha}|}. \end{align*}
Now, we apply the triangle inequality to the numerator and denominator: $|1 - \overline{\beta} \alpha| \leq |1| + |\overline{\beta} \alpha| = 1 + |\alpha|$, and $|\overline{\beta} - \overline{\alpha}| \geq ||\overline{\beta}| - |\overline{\alpha}|| = |1 - |\alpha|| = 1 - |\alpha|$ (since $|\alpha|<1$). Thus, we have
\begin{align*} |\gamma| &\leq \frac{1 + |\alpha|}{1 - |\alpha|}. \end{align*}
To maximize $|\gamma|$, we need to maximize $\frac{1 + |\alpha|}{1 - |\alpha|}$. This is equivalent to maximizing $\frac{1 + x}{1 - x}$ for $0<x<1$, which is achieved when $x=|\alpha|=1/2$. Therefore, the maximum value of $|\gamma|$ is $\frac{1 + 1/2}{1 - 1/2} = 3$. </think> <answer> 3 </answer>
